# Changelog (engine/v1/)

## Correction: branches decal density/speckSize values

**What:** The prior entry below documented the `branches` scatterType's two decal `detail.density`/`detail.speckSize` values as corrected to `4`/`0.15`. That was itself wrong — `compositeShapeDecal`'s `density: mesh.detail.texture.density * ct.detail.density` formula discards the referenced decal preset's own literal density entirely; it only scales against the consuming part's own base-texture density (`branches`' `wood-stump` block, density `1`). `4` was therefore an unprincipled 4x amplification, not a match to `static`'s own density. User corrected the two decals to `1`/`1` and `1.5`/`0.5` via manual runtime testing, confirmed working.

**Why:** `1` is the neutral/no-op value for this multiplier, consistent with every other density/speckSize multiplier in the engine.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json).

## Scatter decal fixes: cylinder cap projection, template-singleton cloning, per-frame precompute

**What:** Follow-up fix pass on the instanced scatter-decal feature (see the entry below). Fixed a correctness bug where cylinder/capsule cap decals (e.g. the `branches` scatterType's cut-cross-section circles) rendered collapsed onto the primitive's rim instead of flat on the cap — the shared decal projection GLSL always radially projected regardless of `local.y`. Fixed via a new `resolveDecalShapeCode(baseShapeCode, side)` that substitutes a flat shape code for cylinder `top`/`bottom` decals, applied at both the per-mesh and instanced decal call sites; the shared GLSL itself is untouched, so sphere/capsule projection is unaffected. Fixed a `UNIT_INSTANCING.md` deviation where the scatter batch path stored and mutated the `textures.json` decal template singleton by reference instead of cloning it, via a new `cloneScatterDecal` in `NewScatter.js` mirroring `NewTemplate.js`'s `cloneTexture`. Fixed a per-frame performance issue where decal placement matrices and the decal-bearing batch list were rebuilt every frame from fully static data — now precomputed once in `buildScatterInstanceBuffers`. Also collapsed 5 duplicated blocks introduced by the original feature (instanced VAO setup, decal blend-state setup, decal display-field init, decal-transform Unit-canonicalization, batch-key composition) into shared helpers. Separately, corrected two content bugs in the `branches` scatterType's decal authoring in `textures.json`: `detail.density`/`detail.speckSize` were literal `null` (zeroing the noise-speck count via multiplication, the actual reason the decals appeared invisible even after increasing scale), corrected to `4`/`0.15`; and `localTransform.scale` was reset from a debugging-inflated `{1,1,1}` back to `{0.1,0.1,1}` matching the branch cylinder's actual cap diameter.

**Why:** ERA and DRYAD audited the original feature and found the cap-projection bug (high severity — the feature's only real content wouldn't render correctly), the singleton-mutation deviation, and the per-frame cost; all three were fixed and independently re-verified clean by both agents.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/handlers/Render.js`](engine/v1/handlers/Render.js) (`resolveDecalShapeCode`, `buildInstancedVao`, `beginDecalState`/`endDecalState`, `buildScatterInstanceBuffers` precompute), [`engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js) (`cloneScatterDecal`, `batchKey`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`InitializeDecalDisplay`), [`engine/v1/builder/templates/Instance.js`](engine/v1/builder/templates/Instance.js) (`canonicalizeDecalTransforms`), [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json).

## Instanced decal rendering for scatter batches

**What:** Added `customTextures`/shape-decal rendering support to the instanced scatter batch path (`generateObjectScatterBatches`), which previously silently dropped any decal authored on a scatter part. Batch identity now incorporates a decal signature (`ComputeCustomTextureSignature`) so two parts with different decal authoring never collapse into one batch. Decal textures bake and register once per batch (reusing `collectCustomTextures`/`compositeShapeDecal` via a small pseudo-mesh shim), and a new instanced decal shader/VAO/draw pass (`createScatterDecalProgram`, `drawScatterDecalPass`) draws one `drawElementsInstanced` call per decal per batch, reusing the batch's existing per-instance model-matrix buffer rather than uploading per-instance decal data. Also fixed a Unit-instancing gap in `builder/templates/Instance.js` — scatter template decal `localTransform.position`/`rotation` were never canonicalized to `UnitVector3`/`Unit`, which this feature now depends on.

**Why:** Scatter batches (the only path actually used for terrain/obstacle scatter) had never carried decal data end-to-end — decals only worked on the unused individual-mesh scatter path (`generateObjectScatter`/`BuildObject`). This closes that gap so authored scatter decals (e.g. the `branches` scatterType's cut-cross-section circles) actually render, without falling back to per-instance decal draw calls that would defeat scatter batching.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js) (`generateObjectScatterBatches` batch key + `customTextures`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`ComputeCustomTextureSignature`, `collectTextureUsage` scatterBatches loop), [`engine/v1/builder/templates/Instance.js`](engine/v1/builder/templates/Instance.js) (`instanceScatterTemplates` decal canonicalization), [`engine/v1/handlers/Render.js`](engine/v1/handlers/Render.js) (`decalSurfaceProjectionGLSL`, `createScatterDecalProgram`, `buildScatterDecalVao`, `buildScatterInstanceBuffers`, `drawScatterDecalPass`, render loop wiring).

## twigs → branches rename, first authored shape-decal customTextures

**What:** Renamed the `twigs` scatterType to `branches` in `textures.json` (key, id, and its one `testGame/levels/levels.json` reference). Added two shape-decal `customTextures` to the branches part's cylinder — `side: "top"` and `side: "bottom"`, both `shape: "circle"` — covering the cylinder's flat end caps to read as the branch's cut cross-section. Both use `detail.baseTextureID: "static"` with a lighter-brown primary/secondary color override, distinct from the branch's own darker `wood-stump` bark texture.

**Why:** User request — "twigs" better named "branches"; the flat cylinder ends read as bare geometry without a decal representing the cut wood grain.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json), [`engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json`](engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json). This is the first authored use of the `texture.custom`/`customTextures` decal array anywhere in `engine/v1`/`testGame` — no prior template exercised it.

## Foliage/wood texture repoints, wood-stump → wood-planks split, new static preset

**What:** In `textures.json`: repointed `scatterTypes.bushes`' sphere part and both `scatterTypes.leaves` parts from `grassland` to `tree-leaves-green` (the leaves' brown color override is untouched). Renamed the `wood-stump` preset (stripes, plank look) to `wood-planks`, updated its one remaining consumer (`scatterTypes.twigs`), and added a *new* `wood-stump` preset (noise, darker brown, density 3, speckSize 0.3) that `scatterTypes.bushes`' stump part now resolves to unchanged. Added a `static` preset (noise, size 256, density 4, speckSize 0.15, white/black) as a general-purpose fine-grain texture other presets can recolor via override. Updated the 5 remaining `wood-stump` references in `testGame/levels/levels.json` and `testGame/levels/entities.json` to `wood-planks`.

**Why:** User request — bushes/leaves foliage should read as tree foliage rather than ground grass; the old `wood-stump` preset actually looked like planks, so the name was wrong for its stripe pattern and freed up for an authentically stump-like noise texture; `static` was requested as a reusable fine-grain base for future color-override textures.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json), [`engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json`](engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json), [`engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json`](engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json).

## grass-blade / tree-leaves-green textures, grass-soft → grassland rename

**What:** Added a `"linear"` pattern case to `drawPattern()` in `NewTexture.js` — a vertical two-color gradient, structurally modeled on the existing `"radial"` case. Authored two new texture presets in `textures.json`: `grass-blade` (uses `linear`, dark base to light tip) and `tree-leaves-green` (uses `noise`, darker/more desaturated than the ground grass texture). Renamed the `grass-soft` preset to `grassland` (key, `id` field, and every `scatterTypes` reference), repointed `scatterTypes.grass`'s 5 cylinder parts to the new `grass-blade` id, and updated the one `testGame/levels/levels.json` reference to `grassland`. The `linear` case uses a 4-stop gradient (matching midtone colors at v=0 and v=1, with primary/secondary at the 0.1/0.9 margins) rather than a plain 2-stop gradient, because scatter batch geometry samples raw non-normalized local-space UVs under `GL_REPEAT` — a plain 2-stop gradient only ever sampled its outer ~20% at each end with a hard color seam at the blade's mid-height.

**Why:** User requested the two new textures, the scatter grass cylinders switched onto the new blade texture, and the `grass-soft` → `grassland` rename. The seamless 4-stop scheme was needed once testing (via ERA/DRYAD audit) surfaced that the naive 2-stop gradient didn't actually render as a smooth ramp given how scatter UVs wrap.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`drawPattern`, `linear` case), [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json), [`engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json`](engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json).

## Texture color alpha fix, opacity removal, color/texture.color promotion

**What:** Fixed alpha being silently dropped during procedural texture generation — `RgbaToHex`/`parseHexColor` now round-trip through 8-digit hex instead of 6-digit. Deleted `generatedTexture.opacity`, deriving `mesh.material.transparent` from `primary.a`/`secondary.a` instead (`material.opacity` is now a constant `1`; alpha lives in the baked texture pixels). Promoted `color` (object-level) and `texture.color` as the modern, non-deprecated authoring surface; `primary`/`secondary` remain supported but are now `deprecated: true`. Replaced the undocumented pre-schema `hoistObjectColor` hack with real schema fields (`levelPart.color`, `levelObject.color`, `generatedTexture.color`) and a post-normalization fold.

**Why:** Alpha on `primary`/`secondary`/texture colors never worked — the hex round-trip had no room for it, and `generatedTexture.opacity` existed purely as a workaround scalar for that bug. The render shader already multiplies per-texel alpha into the tint uniform, so fixing generation was sufficient. The `color`/`primary`/`secondary` naming was also inconsistent: one authoring path (`hoistObjectColor`) was undocumented and bypassed schema validation entirely.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`RgbaToHex`/`parseHexColor`), [`engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json) (`generatedTexture.opacity` removed; `generatedTexture.color`/`levelPart.color`/`levelObject.color` added; `secondary` marked deprecated), [`engine/v1/core/normalize.js`](engine/v1/core/normalize.js) (`hoistObjectColor` removed, `foldGeneratedColorAlias` added, `normalizePart`/`normalizeLevelObject` fold `color` into `primary`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js) (`material.transparent`/`.opacity` derivation), [`engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js) (instance tint alpha), plus content migration in `engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json`.

## Texture color alpha fix — dedup follow-up

**What:** Resolved the two duplication findings from the same-day audit of the change above. `normalize.js`'s `foldGeneratedColorAlias` was generalized into `foldColorAlias(source, sourceKey, dest, destKey)`, used at all four fold call sites (`normalizeTexture`, `normalizeOverrideTexture`, `normalizePart`, `normalizeLevelObject`); this also standardized fold behavior to always delete the source key, removing a null-handling inconsistency between the texture-layer and part/object-layer folds. The duplicated transparency predicate in `NewObject.js`'s two mesh-construction branches was extracted into a new exported `IsTextureTransparent(generatedTexture)` in `NewTexture.js`. Also stripped 45 now-schema-dead `"opacity": 1` keys left over in content JSON after the prior pass deleted `generatedTexture.opacity`.

**Why:** Author-approved cleanup of duplication flagged during review, on the grounds that a transparency check is likely reusable elsewhere and the fold pattern already had one prior extraction (`foldGeneratedColorAlias`) to be consistent with.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/core/normalize.js`](engine/v1/core/normalize.js) (`foldColorAlias`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`IsTextureTransparent`, exported), [`engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js) (both call sites), `engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json` (dead-key removal).

## Texture color alpha fix — water transparency regression fix

**What:** Water rendered fully opaque instead of translucent, a regression introduced by the two changes above. Fixed by setting `primary.a`/`secondary.a` directly to the former `opacity` value (`0.5`) on the water body and water top texture blocks in `buildWaterVisualMeshes`, and removing the now-dead `opacity` keys there and in `buildTriggerMesh`'s trigger-visualization texture block (harmless there — `primary` was already the same object reference as the trigger's `color`, so alpha already flowed correctly — but the key was dead and removed for cleanliness).

**Why:** `engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js` constructs its `texture.generated` object literals directly and passes them straight into `BuildObject`, bypassing `core/normalize.js` entirely — so this direct-construction path was never touched by the fold/deprecation machinery from the original pass and still carried the old `opacity` key, silently dead once `NewObject.js` stopped reading it. Two of the three blocks hardcoded `primary.a`/`secondary.a` to `1` while relying on the dead `opacity: 0.5` for the actual translucency. Caught by the user's own visual testing, not ARGUS.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js) (`buildWaterVisualMeshes`, `buildTriggerMesh`).

## Texture color alpha fix — primary/secondary double-compositing bug

**What:** `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas` in `engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js` fill the canvas with `primary`, then draw `secondary` specks/blocks on top using the canvas default `globalCompositeOperation` (`source-over`). With real per-pixel alpha now baked into the canvas, `source-over` blends `secondary` over the already-semi-transparent `primary` pixel beneath it instead of replacing it — every speck/tile/stripe/grid region ends up as a mixed primary+secondary color at a *compounded* alpha (two colors both at `0.5` alpha compound to `0.75` via Porter-Duff over), rather than pure `secondary` at its own authored alpha. This read visually as a muddied/greenish hue and general oversaturation in patterned regions (most visible on water). Fixed by setting `ctx.globalCompositeOperation = "copy"` at the start of both functions, so every draw replaces the destination pixel outright — each pixel ends up as exactly one authored color at exactly that color's own alpha.

**Why:** This bug was invisible before the alpha fix — when `primary`/`secondary` alpha was always forced to `1`, `source-over` and `copy` behave identically, so `secondary` always cleanly overwrote `primary` regardless. It only became visible once alpha started carrying real values. Root-caused directly from a user visual report (screenshots of before/after) after an initial incorrect hypothesis (background/fog color bleeding through) was ruled out by the user.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`). Not yet visually re-verified by the user — pending.

## Texture color alpha fix — correction to the double-compositing fix above

**What:** The `"copy"` fix in the entry above was itself wrong and caused a severe regression (level rendering broken across the board — reported by the user as magenta shapes and checkerboard chaos). `globalCompositeOperation = "copy"` does not mean "replace only within the newly drawn shape" — it replaces the *entire* canvas with just that draw call's shape, clearing everything outside it (including all earlier draws) to transparent. Since `tiles`/`stripes`/`noise`/`grid` draw `secondary` one shape at a time in a loop (up to 16000 times for `noise`), every subsequent draw wiped out everything before it, including the `primary` base fill. Replaced with a `fillReplacing(ctx, drawFn)` helper: runs `drawFn` once under `destination-out` with an opaque mask (punches a hole matching the shape's exact footprint, safe to repeat since it only affects pixels the shape covers) and once under `source-over` with the real fill style (paints the real color into that now-clean hole). `drawShape` now wraps its whole body in `fillReplacing` (covers the `noise` pattern in both functions, since they share it); the raw `fillRect` calls in `tiles`/`stripes`/`grid` are each wrapped individually. `radial` and the initial `primary` fill are untouched — both are single full-canvas draws, unaffected either way.

**Why:** Wrong assumption about Canvas 2D compositing semantics, asserted as a fix without verifying the spec. Doubles draw-call count in dense patterns, but this is one-time bake cost, not per-frame.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`fillReplacing`, `drawShape`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`). Not yet visually re-verified by the user — pending, and given the previous attempt looked correct but wasn't, this should not be treated as confirmed until it actually renders correctly.

## Texture presets converted to rgba; water authored as real presets

**What:** The `textures` block of the texture preset library now authors `primary`/`secondary` as `{r,g,b,a}` objects instead of 6-digit hex strings, matching the convention the rest of the engine (and the same file's `scatterTypes` block) already used. `parseHexColor` and `RgbaToHex` are deleted; a module-scope `rgbaToCss` feeds the two canvas-paint sites, and `formatColorKey` — extracted from the `fmt` arrow formerly inside `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` — is now shared with `buildFaceTextureSignature`. `shape` became a valid preset field, honored on both the registry path and the per-face path. The zero-consumer `water-surface` preset was repurposed as `underwater` (water body); `sea-surface` was retargeted exclusively at the water top; `buildWaterVisualMeshes` now references both by id with neutral overrides instead of carrying inline color and pattern literals. The water top was retuned: `density` 1 → 0.35 (foam coverage 63.2% → 29.5%), base blue darkened, foam set near-white.

**Why:** The hex round-trip converted per-instance rgba overrides *to* hex and back to a CSS `rgba()` string, quantizing every channel to 8 bits for no reason — it was the original cause of the alpha loss fixed earlier. Authoring the water in `NewLevel.js` as JS literals meant two meshes shared one preset while overriding nearly all of it, leaving the preset's own colors unreachable. The "too much white" symptom was a misdiagnosis of `speckSize`: noise coverage is `1 - exp(-density × shapeFactor)`, in which `speckSize` cancels out entirely and only sets grain size, so `density` is the sole control over the blue/foam split.

**Note — one silent bug averted:** `buildFaceTextureSignature` built its `colorKey` by interpolating the two blueprint colors directly into a template literal. Once those became objects both would have stringified to `[object Object]`, collapsing the key so that any two noise faces differing only by color would share one baked canvas through the `getOrBuildFaceTexture` memo gate. Routing both through `formatColorKey` closes it.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json) (all 11 preset entries), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`rgbaToCss`, `formatColorKey`, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`, `createTextureRegistry`, `ResolveNoiseFaceBlueprint`, `compositeShapeDecal`, `buildFaceTextureSignature`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js) (`buildWaterVisualMeshes`). Colour values are starting points for the author's visual tuning, not final.

## `sea-surface` figure/ground inversion

**What:** Swapped `primary` and `secondary` on the `sea-surface` preset, so the base fill is now the near-white foam and the specks are the water blue; that blue was also deepened to `{ 0.09, 0.28, 0.5 }`. Data-only — no code changed, and the per-instance `density` override in `buildWaterVisualMeshes` was deliberately left alone (the author is tuning it live).

**Why:** The water top read as sky-with-clouds rather than ocean. At roughly even coverage the color drawn as **discrete specks** reads as figure and the base fill reads as ground, so white blobs over blue read as objects sitting on a surface. Inverted, the white becomes the connected *interstitial* network between blue patches — which is how real ocean foam behaves, foam being the gaps between water rather than objects on top of it. The blue was additionally too pale to read as ocean depth.

**Consequence for authoring:** `density` on this preset has inverted meaning. Coverage is still `1 - exp(-density × shapeFactor)` and still describes the **secondary** color, but secondary is now the blue — so raising `density` now increases *blue* coverage, where previously it increased foam.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json) (`sea-surface`). This is the one preset where `primary` is deliberately not the dominant/"own" color of the surface — do not "correct" it back.

## `fillReplacing` erase pass restricted to translucent paint

**What:** `fillReplacing` gained a `replace` parameter and short-circuits to a single plain draw when it is false. The flag is derived once per bake as `secondary.a < 1`, in both `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`, and threaded through `drawShape` (new parameter) and the three raw `fillRect` sites in the `tiles`/`stripes`/`grid` branches. `radial` is untouched — it never used the helper.

**Why:** The erase-then-paint pair was damaging antialiased speck rims on fully-opaque textures. At an edge pixel with coverage `c` over an opaque base it yields `c + (1-c)²` alpha — `0.75` at `c = 0.5` — where plain `source-over` yields a correct `1`. This put pinholes in the speck edges of all 9 opaque presets (visible on `grass-soft`). The erase pass exists only to stop a *translucent* secondary compounding with the primary beneath it; against opaque paint `source-over` already covers the base pixel completely, so the erase achieves nothing and only eats the rim. The two water presets (`underwater`, `sea-surface`) still take the erase path, preserving the deliberate edge artifact that supplies the water body's entire visible variation.

**Settled semantics:** a translucent `secondary` over an opaque `primary` means "this region is partly transparent and shows the scene behind it", not "this region blends its color into the primary". That is the correct reading now that texture alpha is baked per-texel and composited against the scene, and it is what the water depends on.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`fillReplacing`, `drawShape`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`).

## Author-controlled `compositeMode`; texture-id collision fix

**What:** New `generatedTexture.compositeMode` field (`"replace"` / `"overlay"` / `null`), authorable both as a preset field and as a per-instance override. `"replace"` erases before painting so a translucent element fully replaces the base pixel; `"overlay"` paints plainly so it blends and compounds with what is beneath; `null` derives from `secondary.a < 1`, the behaviour that predates the field. A module-scope `resolveReplace` now supplies the boolean that `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas` previously each derived inline. The field is threaded through `createUsageEntry`, `registerTextureUsage`, `textureRegistrationOptions`, `createTextureRegistry`'s spread, and `ResolveNoiseFaceBlueprint`, and enters `buildFaceTextureSignature`'s `colorKey`.

`ComputeGeneratedTextureID` was rewritten in the same pass: it now appends a segment for every field that changes the baked canvas (`p=`, `s=`, `sh=`, `cm=`, `d=`, `ss=`) and its early return is gone.

**Why:** Deriving the composite behaviour from alpha is a sound default but made deliberate blending of two translucent colors unreachable. Separately, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` previously returned the bare preset id unless a *color* override existed, so `shape`/`density`/`speckSize` overrides never reached the texture id — and because `registerTextureUsage` is last-writer-wins on a shared registry entry, two meshes on one preset differing only in those rendered with whichever registered last. Dropping the early return costs nothing: `join("::")` on a single-element array already yields the bare id, so neutral content keeps its original key and only previously-colliding content gains distinct entries.

**Note — no fallback anywhere:** engine-owned template JSON bypasses schema fallbacks, so the key would have been *absent* there while payload data has it as `null`. Absorbing that with `?? null` is forbidden (`FORBIDDEN_DEFENSIVE_CHECKS.md` §1, §3B, §5A). Instead all 92 authoring sites were populated explicitly — 78 `texture.generated` data literals, the 3 synthesized in `NewLevel.js`, and the 11 presets — so the field is genuinely guaranteed and read directly.

**Also:** the 2026-08-18 `opacity` removal had missed engine template JSON entirely; 67 dead `texture.generated.opacity` keys were stripped here. `texture.custom[].opacity` on image decals is live (handled off-schema in `normalize.js`) and all 29 were preserved.

**Where:** [`canonSchemas.json`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json) (`generatedTexture.compositeMode`), [`NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`resolveReplace`, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID`, `buildFaceTextureSignature`, the usage/registry/face-blueprint chain), [`NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js), [`templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json), plus `player/characters.json`, `templates/characters.json`, `templates/particles.json`, `templates/levels.json`. `templates/terrain.json` and `obstacles.json` are `{}` today, so whoever first authors a texture there must include the key.

## `Particle` debug category; bounds-refresh gate widened

**What:** Particles now have their own debug bucket across all three overlays. `CONFIG.DEBUG.LEVELS` gained `BoundingBox.Particle`, `BoundingBox.ParticlePart`, `DetailedBounds.Particle` and `Trails.Particle` (all default `false`), with matching colour-table entries in `Render.js` — a consistent purple identity across bounding boxes, detailed bounds and velocity trails. `classifyEntityTrailType` gained an `includes("particle")` branch, as did both copies of `classifyEntityType` in `NewLevel.js` (`buildSceneBoundingBoxes` and `buildSceneDetailedBounds`).

Separately, `shouldRefreshBoundingBoxes` was rewritten to enumerate both flag groups via `Object.entries`/`Object.values` (excluding the nested `Grid` config object) instead of testing a hardcoded ten-flag `||` chain.

**Why:** No `Particle` debug category existed anywhere. Particle groups are ordinary entities (`type: "particle"`, in `sceneGraph.entities`), and all three classifiers are `type.includes(...)` ladders, so each fell through to a bucket belonging to something else — trails to `"Enemies"`, bounds to `"Entity"`/`"EntityPart"`. Nothing rendered incorrectly; what broke was filterability. There was no way to see enemy trails without particle trails, or entity boxes without particle boxes, and no way to inspect particles specifically — testGame's 40-count spark burst resolves to 8–20 groups of 2–5 parts, so `BoundingBox.EntityPart` buried the real entities under roughly 100 particle boxes per burst.

The gate rewrite fixes an adjacent bug found while tracing that: `shouldRefreshBoundingBoxes` gates `RefreshSceneBoundingBoxes`, which rebuilds **both** `debugBoundingBoxes` and `debug.detailedBounds`, but it only tested `BoundingBox.*` flags and never `DetailedBounds.*`. Every `BoundingBox` flag off with `DetailedBounds.Entity` on would have frozen detailed bounds at level-build state permanently — masked in practice only because `BoundingBox.Terrain` and `.Player` default `true`. Enumerating rather than appending two more keys to a list that had just demonstrated it goes stale also makes the function self-maintaining, and matches the `Object.values(...).some(Boolean)` idiom `drawVelocityTrails` already used. Behaviour is identical under default config.

**Correction to the record:** the deferral this resolves claimed the trail mis-bucketing affected only `"full"`-mode particles. It affected all of them — every physics mode maintains a live `entity.velocity` (`"none"` integrates position from it, `"simple"` applies gravity to it, and every particle re-seeds it on recycle), and `drawVelocityTrails` reads it straight off the scene graph with no mode check.

**Where:** [`config.js`](engine/v1/core/config.js) (`DEBUG.LEVELS.BoundingBox`, `.DetailedBounds`, `.Trails`), [`Render.js`](engine/v1/handlers/Render.js) (`boundingBoxTypeColors`, `detailedBoundsTypeColors`, `trailTypeColors`, `classifyEntityTrailType`), [`NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js) (both `classifyEntityType` closures), [`handlers/game/Level.js`](engine/v1/handlers/game/Level.js) (`shouldRefreshBoundingBoxes`). Every bucket name is consumed by dictionary lookup, so a new category needs exactly one config flag plus one colour entry — these four sites are the whole surface.

## Entity model re-pose skipped when the transform did not move

**What:** `applyModelPose` now records `model.posedTransform` — a snapshot of the root transform the mesh hierarchy currently reflects. `UpdateEntityModelFromTransform` compares that snapshot against `entity.transform` and returns immediately when they match, skipping `refreshEntityDerivedState` entirely. Two module-scope helpers (`vector3Matches`, `poseMatchesTransform`) carry the nine-float comparison.

Its two former root-sync lines are gone as dead work (see below); only the scale re-alias remains. `syncEntityMeshes` in `handlers/game/Level.js` is reduced to three lines: an `if (entity.model)` guard and its unreachable else-branch were removed, along with a per-frame `entity.mesh = entity.model.parts[0].mesh` re-assignment of an already-identical reference.

**Why:** `refreshEntityDerivedState` is the expensive unit — a recursive hierarchy walk with per-part `ComposeTransform`, mesh transform writes and `UpdateObjectWorldAabb`, then `computeEntityAabb` (two vector clones per part), `computeExpandedAabb`, and `computeDetailedBoundsForEntity`. Nothing checked whether the entity had moved, and `runPhysicsLoop` calls `UpdateEntityModelFromTransform` up to six times per physics entity per frame before `syncEntityMeshes` calls it once more unconditionally for every entity in the frame tail.

`entity.model` is guaranteed: every push into `sceneGraph.entities` is a `BuildEntity` product, and `BuildEntity` always produces a model — so the guard was a `FORBIDDEN_DEFENSIVE_CHECKS` violation whose else-branch (three per-frame vector clones) could never run. `entity.mesh` is set to `model.parts[0].mesh` at build and nothing reassigns `part.mesh` after `BuildObject` creates it.

**Note — `entity.transform` aliases `model.rootTransform`:** `BuildEntity` assembles `transform` from `rootTrans.position`/`.rotation`/`.scale` where `rootTrans` **is** `model.rootTransform`, so the old position and rotation sync lines were setting objects to themselves. The scale line is genuinely load-bearing: `NewParticles.js` *replaces* `entity.transform.scale` with a new object on shrink and on recycle, breaking the alias for that one field. This aliasing is also why the check compares against a separate snapshot rather than against `rootTransform` — those are the same objects and would always report "unchanged".

Exact float equality is deliberate: a false negative costs one redundant recompute, never a stale model.

**Correction to the record:** the deferral this resolves claimed each particle group pays a re-pose "whether or not it moved". Particles in flight move every frame — `"none"` integrates position in `advance`, `"simple"`/`"full"` are displaced by the physics pipeline, and `"shrink"` re-points scale — so active particles gain little. The real beneficiaries are larger and elsewhere: entities beyond sim distance, which `Update`'s entity loop already skips but `syncEntityMeshes` re-posed every frame regardless; physics entities at rest, whose up-to-six syncs collapse to only those following an actual change; the frame-tail sync for anything physics already synced; and particle group creation, where the sync immediately after `BuildEntity` is now free.

**Where:** [`NewEntity.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewEntity.js) (`vector3Matches`, `poseMatchesTransform`, `applyModelPose`, `UpdateEntityModelFromTransform`), [`handlers/game/Level.js`](engine/v1/handlers/game/Level.js) (`syncEntityMeshes`). Verified that no runtime path invalidates derived state without changing the root transform — `part.localTransform` is written only by `ResetEntityToDefaultPose` (which calls `refreshEntityDerivedState` directly and re-records the snapshot) and by `normalize.js` at build; `collisionOverride` is written only during normalization; `Animation.js` writes `displayTransform`, not the true pose. Verified in-engine by the author on 2026-08-19 — levels loaded and traversed, no crashes and no behavioural regressions.

## `Animations.Quality` tier casing normalized

**What:** `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE.Animations.Quality` changed from `"high"` to `"High"`, and the inline lookup it feeds in `resolveCorrectionFrames` changed from `{ low, medium, high }` to `{ Low, Medium, High }`. Two sites, no behaviour change — default config resolves to a `qualityScale` of `1` before and after.

**Why:** `Animations.Quality` was the only `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE` tier speaking lowercase; `TerrainScatter`, `RenderDistance`, `SimDistance` and `Particles` all use capitalized `"High"`/`"Medium"`/`"Low"`. The subtree now has one vocabulary. This was the adjacent cleanup surfaced by the tier-consolidation analysis of the same day, and is a prerequisite for any future consolidation of the tier readers.

**Boundary — do not "fix" the remaining lowercase tiers:** every other `"low"`/`"medium"`/`"high"` string in the engine belongs to `complexity`, which is a *payload schema* field, not a config tier. [`canonSchemas.json:1303`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json#L1303) and [`:1521`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json#L1521) declare it with `allowedValues: ["low", "medium", "high", ""]` and `fallback: "medium"`; it is authored lowercase across `builder/templates/*.json` and consumed by the switches at [`NewObject.js:114-147`](engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js#L114-L147). Payload vocabulary is lowercase, `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE` tier vocabulary is capitalized — two deliberately separate vocabularies as of this change.

**Where:** [`config.js:100`](engine/v1/core/config.js#L100), [`Animation.js:64`](engine/v1/handlers/game/Animation.js#L64). Nothing writes `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE` at runtime, `testGame` never touches it, and `ini.js` does not expose it, so producer and consumer were the complete set.

## Particle groups up to 10 parts; count-scaled minimum group size

**What:** `maxGroupSize` rose from 5 to 10, and the minimum group size became a function of emission count rather than a constant — `minGroupSizeFor(count)` returns 5 above 20, 3 above 10, and 2 otherwise. `jitterGroupSizes` now takes `minSize` as a parameter; `resolveGroupSizes` resolves it once per call.

`groupLayouts` is now built once at module import by a loop from 1 to `maxGroupSize`. The hand-tuned layouts for sizes 1–5 are preserved verbatim as `tunedLayouts` and used as-is; sizes past that are generated by `sphereLayout`, a golden-angle (Fibonacci) sphere distribution. Lookup remains a plain index, exactly as before — nothing is computed per call. The tuned/generated boundary derives from `Object.keys(tunedLayouts).length`, so raising `maxGroupSize` again needs no further edits.

**Why:** testGame's 40-count spark burst was resolving into a mean of ~14 particle entities per landing, each paying full physics and per-frame model sync. Mean entity count for that burst is now ~5.97, a 57% reduction. Emissions below 8 are untouched — they take the separate `count < 3` and `count <= 7` branches.

**Note — two things the deferral got wrong.** It described this as "a small additive change to `groupLayouts`". Neither half held. First, raising `maxGroupSize` *alone* achieves almost nothing: group count is `randomInt(max(3, ceil(count / maxGroupSize)), floor(count / minGroupSize))`, whose **upper** bound is driven by the minimum, not the maximum — raising the cap only lowers the floor, moving the 40-count mean from ~14 to ~12. The count-scaled minimum is where the actual saving lives. Second, `groupLayouts` is indexed by exact size, so raising the cap to 10 makes sizes 6–9 reachable too (`balancedSplit(40, 5)` yields `[8,8,8,8,8]`); a missing `groupLayouts[8]` would have crashed on `.map`. Five new layouts were mandatory, not one.

**Intended, not a bug:** entity count is non-monotonic at the two tier boundaries — `count: 10` averages 4.00 entities while `count: 11` averages 3.00, and `count: 20` averages 4.50 while `count: 21` averages 3.50. Crossing a threshold always makes an emission cheaper, never more expensive.

**Where:** [`NewParticles.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewParticles.js) (`maxGroupSize`, `tunedLayouts`, `goldenAngle`, `sphereLayout`, `groupLayouts`, `minGroupSizeFor`, `jitterGroupSizes`, `resolveGroupSizes`). Group-size math was exhaustively exercised across the full schema range for `count` (1–50) at 20000 runs each: every produced size stayed within 1–10 and part totals always summed exactly to `count`, so `groupLayouts[size]` is defined by construction. Generated offsets are unit magnitude, matching the tuned set's 0.8–1.1 range, with closest-pair separation near 1.0 even at size 10 so parts never overlap. Verified in-engine by the author on 2026-08-19 — behaves as expected, despite changing the look of any emission of 8 or more (fewer, chunkier groups mean fewer independent trajectories per burst).

## Per-face meshes no longer bake an unbindable registry texture

**What:** `collectMesh` now returns immediately for any mesh carrying `geometry.faceTextureGroups`, so per-face meshes no longer register a `material.textureID` usage entry. Those entries were baked into the texture registry at level build and could never be bound: `drawMeshList` binds `group.textureID` per face group and `continue`s, so the `mesh.material.textureID` bind below it is unreachable for exactly the meshes that were registering. Eliminates a handful of dead canvas bakes per level, and — where an animated preset is involved — one dead entry per level from the texture animation loop.

**Why:** Raised as a water-specific deferral, but the scope was wider than the entry described. The per-face gate admits any terrain/obstacle/water mesh with a noise pattern and no primitive-supplied UVs, and only spheres supply those, so ordinary noise-textured terrain and obstacle cubes took the same path. Terrain also registers `isTerrain: true`, which scales the dead canvas by up to 8× in `resolveTextureSize` — a 64px preset baking at up to 512×512. Water's two 64×64 entries were the cheapest instance of the problem, not the whole of it. The water top's dead entry additionally enrolled in `Texture.js`'s animation loop (`sea-surface` sets `animation.able: true`), and because that preset sets `holdTime: 0` its hold phase expired instantly, leaving it permanently blending — full-canvas composites every frame and a fresh bake every blend cycle, for pixels nothing displays. All CPU-side: `ensureSceneTexture` is never called for an unbound id, so the dead entries never reached the GPU and their per-frame `dirty` flag was never acted on.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`collectMesh`). Audited before editing, because `registerTextureUsage` is last-writer-wins and its `isTerrain`/`maxSpan` assignment is sticky — had a per-face mesh and a binding mesh shared one generated-texture id, dropping the per-face registration would have shrunk the shared entry's resolved canvas and visibly coarsened the speck scale on the mesh that actually uses it. Every `texture.generated` block in `testGame/levels/levels.json` and `entities.json` was keyed by `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` and bucketed by path: zero overlap between the two sets, so the guard is behaviourally inert for current content. Entity parts return early in `BuildObject` and never take the per-face path; scatter meshes carry `role: "scatter"` and are likewise unaffected; `scatterBatches` call `registerTextureUsage` directly and bypass `collectMesh` entirely. The guard also introduces no new dereference risk — invisible-mode meshes carry neither `geometry` nor `detail` and are already filtered out by the `mode !== "default"` checks upstream, and void-mode meshes fall through it into the pre-existing `mesh.detail.texture` read unchanged.

## Texture animation moved to the GPU; bakes amortized; shape decals can animate

**What:** The animated-texture crossfade no longer runs on the CPU. `Texture.js` is now a pure state machine — `blendTextureSurfaces` and the `activeSurface` canvas are gone, it no longer writes `textureEntry.source`, and each frame it only advances timers, advances a resumable bake, and sets `blendRatio`. `Render.js` composites instead, drawing `mix(from, to, ratio)` into the entry's existing GL texture through a framebuffer. Every consumer downstream is untouched: all four programs built through `createFoggedTextureFragmentShader`, `drawMeshList`, `drawSortedRuns`, `drawDecalPass` and the scatter draw keep binding exactly one texture per id.

The per-cycle noise bake is now resumable and spread across the animation cycle. `BeginNoiseBake` paints the base fill and returns a job; `AdvanceNoiseBake` draws up to a budget. `Texture.js` budgets by elapsed milliseconds rather than a predicted frame count, so a slow frame draws more and the surface still lands on schedule. Three surfaces are in flight — `fromSurface`, `toSurface`, and a completed `readySurface` lookahead — with a bake always running for whichever slot is free. A bake that overruns its window stretches the cycle instead of spiking, which removes every synchronous runtime bake including the first one after level load.

Shape decals gained animation, which they could not have before: decal registry entries carried no `definition`, so `Texture.js` skipped them unconditionally. A new `animated` flag on `levelCustomTexture.detail` and `levelDecalSource.detail` opts a decal in, and `decalAnimationDefinition` attaches a definition only when the decal opts in *and* its preset's `animation.able` is true. Hold and blend times come from the preset — per-decal speed control is deliberately withheld, matching `shape` and `compositeMode` already being preset-only for decals. All 33 authored decals use `rubber-skin`, which is not `animation.able`, so this ships with no visual change until something opts in.

Two removals came with it. The `Math.min(16000, ...)` cap on the *face-path* `speckCount` is gone — the area term feeding it was uncapped, so past roughly 1100×1100 px (~550 CNU at `textureScale` 2) speck count stopped tracking area and noise silently thinned as a surface grew; amortization is what makes lifting it safe. And the `dirty` machinery is deleted outright: once `Texture.js` stopped compositing, nothing raised the flag, leaving a dead re-upload branch in `ensureSceneTexture` and a dead field on three registry initializers.

**Why:** This was the engine's worst per-frame cost and the failure was structural, not a tuning problem. Every frame, for every animated entry, the old path did a `clearRect` plus two full-canvas `drawImage` calls on the JS main thread, then set `dirty`, which drove a full `texImage2D` — a texture *reallocation* that invalidates the texture object and can stall draws already referencing it. stage0 ran three animated entries and spent roughly 186 KB of reallocation per frame to communicate a single float. `sea-surface` sets `holdTime: 0`, so it never paused.

The cost scaled with texture area rather than screen coverage, and per-face canvas dimensions are unbounded: a 500×500 CNU surface at `textureScale` 2 is a million texels composited and 4 MB uploaded *per frame*. Moving the crossfade to the GPU changes what the cost is proportional to — per-frame work becomes O(screen-independent texture area) on hardware built for it, uploads drop to one `texSubImage2D` per cycle, and the outgoing `from` buffer is recycled as the incoming `to` so no reallocation happens at all. Two alternatives were rejected on principle and must not be reintroduced: retuning `sea-surface`'s `holdTime` (changes config, fixes nothing for a game developer) and clamping per-face canvas dimensions (the engine's former behaviour — it prevented the cost but rendered large faces tiled at the clamped size, which looked abysmal). Scope was set by what the engine should support, not by what testGame contains.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/handlers/game/Texture.js`](engine/v1/handlers/game/Texture.js) (whole-file rewrite around `createAnimationStateEntry`/`updateTextureAnimationEntry`), [`engine/v1/handlers/Render.js`](engine/v1/handlers/Render.js) (`createBlendProgram`, `uploadTextureImage`, `ensureSceneTexture`, `ensureBlendQuad`, `ensureAnimatedSources`, `blendAnimatedTextures`, renderer init, `drawScene`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`BeginNoiseBake`, `AdvanceNoiseBake`, `BeginTextureBake`, `decalAnimationDefinition`, `createTextureRegistry`, `BuildFaceTextureData`), [`engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json) (`animated` on both decal `detail` groups), [`engine/v1/player/characters.json`](engine/v1/player/characters.json) (33 decal `detail` blocks populated).

`blendAnimatedTextures` runs in `drawScene` immediately after `syncCanvasSize` and *before* the existing `gl.viewport` call, so the viewport, depth and blend lines already there restore everything it changes — there is no explicit restore, and moving that call breaks it. It skips ids absent from `renderer.textures` (never drawn, so nothing samples the target — this is also what keeps inactive decal source variants free) and entries whose bootstrap bake has not landed. `pendingSurface` is raised only on the steady-state rollover and never on the bootstrap fill, because `ensureAnimatedSources` already uploads both surfaces on creation; raising it in both places would double-upload and leave `from` and `to` on the same image, silently killing the crossfade.

**Two notes for anyone extending this.** `bakeKind` (`"face"` / `"surface"` / `"decal"`) replaced the `isFaceTexture` boolean and is the single discriminator `BeginTextureBake` dispatches on — a fourth bake shape goes there and nowhere else. And the separate `Math.min(16000, ...)` cap inside `drawPattern`'s `noise` case was deliberately **left in place**: `drawPattern` is not resumable and its registry-path sizes are already bounded at 512 by `toPowerOfTwoSize`, so lifting that one would reintroduce exactly the single-frame spike this pass removed.

Verified by `node --check`, a scripted assertion that all 33 decal `detail` blocks carry the new key with the 55 `texture.generated` blocks untouched, and a standalone replica of the state machine exercised across ten timing scenarios at 4000 frames each — steady, jittery, stalling 400–900 ms frames, zero deltas, a 55555-speck surface, and degenerate authoring (`blendTime 0`, `holdTime 0`). Every invariant held. Browser profiling, author visual sign-off and a large-surface stress test are still outstanding — the `texImage2D` stall was reasoned from source and bandwidth arithmetic, not measured.
